<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Scheldt</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Schelde, Escaut</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Scheldt is a river of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, flowing about 350 km from northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. Its estuary gives access to the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>, and for centuries its valley was a frontier and trade route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>'s closure of its mouth after 1585 crippled Antwerp's trade for two centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/scheldt.jpg" alt="Scheldt" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tournai_JPG05.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
